  "step": [
    {
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1,
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "When you're adding a room, getting the framing plan perfect from the start can save you a ton of headaches. Here’s a quick checklist to help you get ready. First, make sure you've got all your permits and zoning approvals sorted. You don't want to start building only to find out you're violating a local code. Next, get clear on the materials that will be used for your framing. Different types of wood or engineered lumber have different strengths and costs, and you want to know what you're paying for. Then, confirm how the new framing will tie into your existing home's structure. This is a critical point for stability and ensuring everything lines up properly. You should also understand what the framing needs to support later on, like heavy roofing materials or multiple stories. This impacts beam sizes and structural design. Finally, confirm any inspection requirements for the framing phase. Knowing what inspectors will be looking for beforehand means you can address potential issues proactively. If any of these steps feel too complex, especially tying into existing structures or dealing with complex load conditions, don't hesitate to reach out to a professional framing contractor. They can guide you through the process and ensure a safe, sound build."
    }
  ],
